# Screening: cut-v2 (director release `screen-cut-v2`, editor 2bdabad), 10:58

## How I watched (read this first)
I am a model, not a person with ears and a screen. I could not play the film in real time or hear it. What I did on `cut-v2-elephants-dream.mp4`:
- **Picture:** looked at frames from the whole file: every 1-2 s over 0:00-0:32, then slate frames at 0:40, 1:40, 3:20, 6:36, then every 2.5-3 s through the credits (9:56-10:58). The middle (0:32-9:55) is slates; I viewed 4 of them and measured the rest with ffmpeg freeze-detect (each slate holds 3-18 s).
- **Sound:** I transcribed the soundtrack with Whisper (small model) as a stand-in for listening, and checked that each of the 75 subtitle cues has the same words speaking at the same time. I also measured loudness and silence (ffmpeg ebur128/silencedetect, RMS per 10 s). Everything below about sound is therefore *what a speech recogniser found and what the meters say*. It is **not** an impression of mood, timbre or music quality, and I make no claim about those.
- Subtitles: I read the `.vtt` and compared timing and reading speed. I did not see the browser player render them (the `index.html` fetch did not complete here).
- I did not watch the original Elephants Dream first.

## What I understood as a first-time viewer
- 0:00-0:30 gives a title, then two men, one terrified older man (Proog) and a younger man (Emo) running on a rusty bridge in a machine of cables, something lashes at them. That is clear and intriguing.
- From 0:30 to 9:55 there is **no film**, only a dark card per shot ("S038 · SC04 · MISSING PICTURE", a timecode and a sentence of what should be on screen, with the spoken line in blue). Because the cards describe the action and print the line, I could follow the story as a *read-along*: Proog is the controlling guide of the machine, "listen, it's not safe", Emo doubts him ("there's nothing there"), they quarrel, Emo breaks away and shouts the Hanging Gardens / Colossus lines, Proog insists "it is there". I understood the relationship and the argument from words and cards. I did not *see* anything, so this is not a viewing of the film.
- 9:55-10:51 credits, 10:51-10:58 black.

## Timecoded notes
- **0:00-0:02** Dark rusted-metal wall, no title yet. Audio starts from near silence (-73 dB) and rises; nothing else to hold me.
- **0:02-0:14** "Elephants Dream" fades in over the wall and sits there ~12 s. Looks good, but 12 s with no movement and no voice is where I'd start to wonder if it's stuck. Held attention: mild. (Meters: bed at about -28 dB the whole time; I can't say what it is.)
- **0:15-0:21** A voice starts over the title/credit card ("At the left we can see... at the right we can see the... head-snarlers. Everything is safe. Perfectly safe.") Recognised words start 14.8, subtitle starts 15.1, they agree. Clear and odd in a good way; the tour-guide tone is a hook. But the picture under it is still the card ("a VideoWorld film production", 0:18-0:24), so the line has nothing to illustrate.
- **0:21-0:25** Card fades through dark to black. 3-4 s of no picture and no speech between the tour-guide line and the next.
- **0:25** Cut to Proog's face close-up, scared, "Emo? Emo!" (heard 25.7, cue 25.2). Strongest moment so far: a face, a name, alarm.
- **0:28-0:30** Wide: Emo scrambles on the bridge, Proog lunges, "Watch out!" This looks like a still with a slow push-in rather than moving action (consecutive frames 0.6 s apart differ only by zoom), so the "danger" is told by the voice, not shown. Still the best-looking bit of the film.
- **0:30 onward** First slate (S004). The drop from picture to a flat grey-black card is abrupt, with the music/ambience running on. I understood within a second that this is a placeholder, only because the card says MISSING PICTURE.
- **0:46-1:08** Speech heard: "Are you hurt?", "I don't think so. You?", "I'm OK. Get up, it's not safe here", "Let's go.", "What's next?", "You'll see... you'll see." All understood, in the right order.
- **1:34-1:38** "Follow me!" then the subtitle "(indistinct mutter as he leaps on)": the recogniser got "ma'am, I'll take care of you", so the mutter is perhaps not as indistinct as captioned, but I can't tell by ear.
- **2:11 "Hurry, Emo!" (L14)**: I could not find those words in the audio; level at that moment is about 4 dB under the surrounding bed (-25.8 vs -21.7 dB). Likely mumbled/buried. Flag for the sound owner to check by ear.
- **2:15-3:19** "Listen, listen!", Emo's "I just want to answer the phone", Proog's lecture ("You have to learn to listen... we could easily die out here... listen to your breathing"). The cards let me follow it; heard in order with good sync. **3:03 "This is not some game."** (L21): not found by the recogniser, same suspicion as 2:11; the neighbouring lines are heard.
- **3:20-4:25 (about 66 s)** no subtitle cue, no recognised speech, loudness drifts -24 to -27 dB, slates change every 5-18 s. This is where I would have drifted off if I were watching a card with no voice. (Ambience only, I can't judge what it is.)
- **4:26-4:52** "Don't you ever get tired of this?" / "clockwork... ground to a pulp... Pulp!" Understood; the Proog speech (L29-L31) is funny and heard throughout.
- **4:52-5:41** About 50 s with no speech; whisper transcribes counting "3, 4, 5, 6 ..." around 5:16-5:43, probably a counting sound effect (or a hallucination; I can't be sure which).
- **5:41-5:45** "Emo, close your eyes. Now!" Not individually confirmed in the audio (the whisper text is "Very well" at 5:44), so check by ear.
- **5:59-6:16** The "what do you see at your left/right" exchange: heard and understood, and it is the first time the film plays like a scene (short, alternating lines).
- **6:40-8:21** The fight. Short alternating lines ("There?" "Yeah." "It isn't safe, Emo." "But..." "No!") with natural gaps, then Emo's accusations (7:24-7:48), the "trap" reversal at 7:52, "Hanging Gardens of Babylon!", "Colossus of Rhodes!". Held me most of any stretch, mainly because of the dialogue. Subtitle timing: only 9 of 75 cues could be matched cleanly at both ends (within 0.5 s); the other lines sit within 3 s of their neighbours, so for those I could only confirm the words are spoken around the cue, not to the frame. Where a line was checked alone, 5 are late by 0.5-1.0 s: L02 "Emo? Emo!" (+0.5 at 25.2), L44 "Yeah." (+0.7 at 6:46), L55 "Yeah?" (+0.9 at 7:10), L56 (+1.0 at 7:11), L67 "No, Emo." (+0.6 at 8:05). I would not call any of them drift.
- **8:50-8:54** "It is there... I'm telling you, Emo... it is..." the last line of dialogue (8:50.3 per the cue; heard 8:50). After this about 60 s of no speech before the credits at 9:55.
- **9:00-9:30** Loudness falls to -27/-28 dB (the quietest block), then lifts at 9:30: a transition. Fine as a lull.
- **9:55-10:51** Credits. They scroll with legible type and a long hold. They list roles with the internal position names under them ("director", "production-designer", "dp", "sound-designer", "editor", "qc", "test-audience") and no personal names for the film's makers; to a viewer this reads like a template, and "Test Screening" and "Quality Control" are not credits an audience expects. The "Based on Elephants Dream (2006)" block with the original voices' names (Cas Jansen, Tygo Gernandt) beside "All picture, voices, music and effects in this film are newly made" is confusing: are those the voices I heard?
- **10:51-10:58** Black, audio falls away to -64 dB by 10:58. Clean end. No stalls, no silence longer than 1.5 s at -45 dB anywhere in the file, no playback breaks.

## Answers to the director's questions
1. **Can I follow what each voice says? Two distinct people?** I could recover most of the 75 lines as words and in order (the exceptions are listed above: 2:11, 3:03, 5:41 and a few one-word lines). Names get garbled by the recogniser ("Proog" as "Krug/Prude/Prug"; "Emo" as "Emote/Emho"), which suggests the name Proog isn't crisply pronounced, but this is a machine, not an ear. For distinctness I only have a crude measure: from sound-feature averages the two speakers' lines separate 88% of the time against 68% for always guessing Proog (41 lines, leave-one-out). That says the two voices are acoustically different. It does not say they sound like two *characters*, and it says nothing about consistency of one man across the film. Please have a person listen to this point.
2. **Mood from music and effects?** I can't judge that. What I can say: the sound is continuous (never silent), loudness sits at -19 to -28 dB with dips at 3:20-4:25, 9:00-9:30 and a fade at the very end, integrated -20.6 LUFS, loudness range 8.3 LU.
3. **Subtitles in time and long enough to read?** Timing agrees with speech wherever I could check it (above), though that check is coarse. Reading speed is high: 29 of 75 cues run above 17 characters per second, 15 above 21 (e.g. L17 "You're not paying attention!" 1.1 s; L73 "And it is here just for you, Proog." 1.5 s, 23.6 cps). Because they follow the voice they can't be held longer without overlapping the next line; in these dense exchanges (4:26, 6:40, 7:24, 8:00-8:20) a viewer reading is the limit. The slates also print the line, which hides this problem for now.
4. **Opening 30 s as a viewer?** It sets a tone, and the Proog face and the bridge make me want more. But the first 15 s are a title with no voice, the voice arrives over a credit card, and the real action only begins at 0:25. Then it stops dead at 0:30. Nothing keeps going to the end of the opening.
5. **Anything that breaks playback?** No: no stalls found, audio runs the full 10:58 and matches the picture length. The only break is that the picture is missing from 0:30 to 9:55.

## Overall
The sound and the dialogue carry the story, and I followed the argument. As a film I did not see it: 1:25 of 10:58 is title, picture and credits and the 9:25 in the middle is cards. Nothing here could be locked as the film. For the next screening, the first things I will look for are whether the picture follows the dialogue at the moments above (2:11, 3:03, 6:40-8:21) and whether the picture moves.
